==============================================================================
2. POSITIVE CONTROL - the known moving person (FMCW cfg A)
==============================================================================
Same estimator shape as the fan analysis: per-channel envelope ->
periodogram -> average across independent channels -> LOCAL measured
floor.  Channels here are independent RANGE BINS spaced by the 0.83 m
MEASURED resolution; for the CW fan they are carrier SUB-BANDS.
Envelope frame rate = ramp rate = 50 Hz.

  moving : 240 chirps (4.80 s), 6 independent range bins | MTI-envelope mean  -20.47 dB | cadence peak 2.09 Hz at  5.57 sigma over the LOCAL measured floor (21 bins searched)
  static : 160 chirps (3.20 s), 6 independent range bins | MTI-envelope mean  -27.24 dB | cadence peak 2.52 Hz at  1.20 sigma over the LOCAL measured floor (14 bins searched)
  fan_on : 240 chirps (4.80 s), 6 independent range bins | MTI-envelope mean  -28.16 dB | cadence peak 2.93 Hz at  2.99 sigma over the LOCAL measured floor (21 bins searched)

  Sanity vs FINDINGS: MTI energy moving - static = +6.77 dB in the 1.0-2.5 m gate (FINDINGS: +6.69 dB / 12 sigma at 1.5 m).  Reproduced.
  Multiple comparisons: 21 cadence bins searched; 5.57 sigma expected by chance 0.0000 times.
  Empty-room static control reaches only 1.20 sigma -> the estimator does not generate its own peaks.
  Recovered 2.09 Hz = a walking cadence of ~2.1 steps/s.

  POSITIVE CONTROL VERDICT: PASS  (5.57 sigma)
  (A first attempt using a Welch-averaged SINGLE-gate envelope reached only
   0.6 sigma on this same person.  Channel averaging is what makes the
   method work, and it is the version applied to the fan.)

==============================================================================
2b. SECOND POSITIVE CONTROL, ENTIRELY IN CW: the 60 Hz mains AM
==============================================================================
The mains harmonics are a real, known amplitude modulation of this CW IF.
With the carrier-domain mains notch DISABLED the pipeline must recover a
60 Hz envelope line.  This validates the CW envelope chain end to end.

  notch OFF      (K=14 sub-bands): 60 Hz envelope line  incoherent   +228.0 sigma   cross-sub-band coherent   +358.8 sigma
  notch +/-5 Hz  (K=14 sub-bands): 60 Hz envelope line  incoherent   +198.5 sigma   cross-sub-band coherent   +357.9 sigma

  -> the CW envelope chain detects a genuine AM at enormous sigma.  CW
     POSITIVE CONTROL: PASS.  It also shows why a 60 Hz envelope line can
     never be called a blade line here: the fan is an electrical load, and
     FINDINGS section 3 measures 35.9 dB of 60 Hz in the EMPTY room.

